1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,200
In Singapore, the boss of the most valuable company on Earth is having dinner with a Chinese spy.

2
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:08,600
He's Jensen Huang, who runs NVIDIA.

3
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:10,600
She's Alice Huang, no relation.

4
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:13,200
And she runs one of his biggest customers.

5
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:14,800
But here's the strange part.

6
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:18,300
Somewhere nearby, American agents are taking pictures.

7
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:20,300
They know exactly who she is.

8
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:22,000
Jensen has no idea.

9
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:23,400
To our customers!

10
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:25,300
To friends!

11
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:29,700
Under that table, her company is buying billions of dollars of his chips.

12
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:32,000
The kind China isn't allowed to have.

13
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,700
This is the story of how China got a spy into that seat.

14
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:37,200
And who was watching.

15
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:39,700
It starts with a phone call from Washington.

16
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:47,600
But to see why that call mattered, you need to know who picked it up.

17
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:50,600
Because the man in the leather jacket didn't start at the top.

18
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:53,100
He started in a bathroom in Kentucky.

19
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:55,600
Jensen's story begins in Taiwan.

20
00:00:55,600 --> 00:01:00,200
At nine, he and his older brother were put on a plane to relatives in America.

21
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:04,200
To pay for a good school, his parents sold almost everything they owned.

22
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:08,200
Oneida Baptist Institute, a boarding school in Kentucky.

23
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:09,400
Very strict.

24
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:11,100
Strict is good.

25
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:13,000
It wasn't a fancy boarding school.

26
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,800
It was a reform school for troubled kids, and the relatives had signed them up by mistake.

27
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:20,700
His brother was sent to work the school's tobacco fields.

28
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:22,500
Jensen got the bathrooms.

29
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:25,900
A hundred teenage boys' worth every single day.

30
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:28,400
I just wish they would be a bit more careful.

31
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:34,100
His roommate was 17, covered in tattoos and knife scars, and taped up from his latest fight.

32
00:01:34,100 --> 00:01:37,500
He was the toughest kid in the school, and he couldn't read.

33
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:38,500
What's this say?

34
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:39,700
I can teach you.

35
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:41,800
Then I teach you the bench press.

36
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:42,500
Deal.

37
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:47,300
Decades later, Jensen gave that school two million dollars for a new building.

38
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:48,800
It's called Huang Hall.

39
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:52,300
The boy who cleaned its bathrooms put his name on the front door.

40
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:55,500
At 15, he took the graveyard shift at Denny's.

41
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:57,300
Dishes first, and then?

42
00:01:57,300 --> 00:01:58,500
Then Busboy.

43
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:01,500
Then, if you're lucky, waiter.

44
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:03,100
And then?

45
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:04,600
There, more dishes.

46
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:09,700
And in 1993, he went back to Denny's, not to work, to start a company.

47
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:16,400
In a corner booth in East San Jose, Jensen and two engineer friends, Chris Malachowski and Curtis Priem,

48
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:18,700
sketched a chip for 3D graphics.

49
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:22,500
He liked the place because it was quieter than home, and the coffee was cheap.

50
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:23,700
Anything else, boys?

51
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:24,900
A graphics chip.

52
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:26,500
We have pancakes.

53
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:28,300
Then, three more coffees.

54
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:34,400
Each founder put in $200, $600, about the price of a used couch,

55
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:37,400
to take on the biggest chip companies in the world.

56
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:38,900
It nearly died.

57
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:45,500
When its Maker Break chip finally shipped in 1997, NVIDIA had about one month of payroll left.

58
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:47,800
The company's unofficial motto became,

59
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:51,000
Our company is 30 days from going out of business.

60
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:52,700
So, how long have we got?

61
00:02:52,700 --> 00:02:54,000
30 days!

62
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:56,500
And if the new chip doesn't sell?

63
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:57,600
29!

64
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:00,700
It sold, about a million of them in four months.

65
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:03,400
And here's the trick that later made it a giant.

66
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:09,100
A normal computer chip is one brilliant chef cooking a whole wedding dinner, one plate at a time.

67
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:15,500
NVIDIA's chip is 10,000 line cooks, each flipping one pancake at the very same moment.

68
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:18,600
For video games, that meant explosions.

69
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:23,000
Then, researchers noticed that artificial intelligence needs exactly that.

70
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Millions of tiny sums, all at once.

71
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,700
ChatGPT was trained on a supercomputer built from tens of thousands of NVIDIA chips.

72
00:03:31,700 --> 00:03:35,900
And suddenly, every AI company on Earth was standing in the same line.

73
00:03:35,900 --> 00:03:37,300
One of everything, please.

74
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:38,100
Take a number!

75
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:39,200
40,000?

76
00:03:40,900 --> 00:03:42,600
That's your place in line!

77
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:48,900
By 2025, about 86 of every $100 spent on AI chips went to NVIDIA.

78
00:03:48,900 --> 00:03:52,100
In 2023, Denny's put a plaque on the booth.

79
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:56,000
It calls it the booth that launched a $1 trillion company.

80
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:02,300
At the party, the boss of that company put on an apron, balanced 10 plates up his arm, and served the tables.

81
00:04:02,300 --> 00:04:03,500
Still remember how?

82
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:04,600
Dishes first.

83
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:10,100
Two years later, NVIDIA became the first company ever worth $5 trillion.

84
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:14,500
Every dollar the three of them put into that booth had become about $8 billion.

85
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:19,700
The kid who scrubbed the toilets in Kentucky now holds the key to artificial intelligence.

86
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:24,000
And when he talks about who should be allowed to buy his chips, Washington listens.

87
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,200
Every AI lab on Earth needs this man.

88
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:28,700
Then, so do we.

89
00:04:28,700 --> 00:04:31,900
That made him the one man China needed most.

90
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,500
October 2022, Washington calls NVIDIA.

91
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:40,800
Your best AI chips can't be sold to China anymore.

92
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:43,300
And the slower ones we made just for China?

93
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:44,400
Those too.

94
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:45,000
China.

95
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:47,700
$17 billion a year.

96
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:50,800
13% of everything we sell was

97
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:53,900
How many chips do we need for a real AI?

98
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:55,400
Tens of thousands.

99
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:56,600
And one costs?

100
00:04:56,600 --> 00:04:58,800
About $30,000.

101
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:00,600
A new car per chip.

102
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:02,200
And how many can we buy?

103
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:02,900
Zero.

104
00:05:02,900 --> 00:05:08,900
By Jensen's own account, NVIDIA's share of China's market went from 95% to zero.

105
00:05:08,900 --> 00:05:10,500
And he told Washington so.

106
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:11,900
Every chance he got.

107
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:13,700
They're nanoseconds behind us.

108
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:15,200
We've got to go compete.

109
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:19,800
So China couldn't buy the chips, but it could get close to the man who makes them

110
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:23,400
and who argues with Washington about who gets to buy them.

111
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:27,600
That's a job for China's Ministry of State Security, the MSS.

112
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:31,200
Picture the FBI and the CIA in one building.

113
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:32,700
It doesn't crack the safe.

114
00:05:32,700 --> 00:05:34,700
It befriends the man with the key.

115
00:05:34,700 --> 00:05:38,600
Nobody outside Beijing has said when Alice Huang was recruited,

116
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:41,600
but an American memo later wrote down her job.

117
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:45,200
This man makes the chips, and Washington listens to him.

118
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:47,300
So what do I steal?

119
00:05:47,300 --> 00:05:48,200
Nothing.

120
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:50,000
You learn what he knows.

121
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:51,800
You help him see things our way.

122
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:53,600
Why would he even talk to me?

123
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:55,500
Because you'll be his customer.

124
00:05:55,500 --> 00:05:56,300
And then?

125
00:05:56,300 --> 00:05:57,600
We make friends.

126
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:01,800
The key belonged to one man, and that man likes everybody.

127
00:06:03,700 --> 00:06:10,800
In 2023, a Chinese gaming and cloud company called Seven Road split off its business outside China,

128
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:13,800
moved it to Singapore, and renamed it Megaspeed.

129
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:16,100
The managing director was Alice Huang.

130
00:06:16,100 --> 00:06:17,600
We make phone games.

131
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:19,800
Now we make supercomputers.

132
00:06:19,800 --> 00:06:20,900
Since when?

133
00:06:20,900 --> 00:06:22,700
Since Singapore.

134
00:06:22,700 --> 00:06:23,600
Address?

135
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:24,600
Singapore.

136
00:06:24,600 --> 00:06:26,600
America trusts Singapore.

137
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:27,600
Business?

138
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:28,800
Cloud computing.

139
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:33,000
A cloud company needs mountains of chips, and nobody asks why.

140
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:34,900
And the chips go where?

141
00:06:34,900 --> 00:06:37,700
Data centers in Malaysia and Indonesia.

142
00:06:37,700 --> 00:06:39,700
Not one chip goes to China.

143
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:42,000
Then how does China use them?

144
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:43,600
It rents them.

145
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:46,600
Imagine a country that isn't allowed to buy cars.

146
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:52,000
So you buy the cars, park them just over the border, and rent out the keys by the hour.

147
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:56,800
Chinese companies, Alibaba among them, rented time on the chips the same way,

148
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:58,800
and the chips never entered China.

149
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:00,500
Can we buy your chips?

150
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:02,800
No, but you can rent them.

151
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:03,700
By the hour?

152
00:07:03,700 --> 00:07:05,000
By the warehouse.

153
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:11,000
At the end of 2023, everything Megaspeed owned came to about $33 million.

154
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:15,500
A nice little company, and not one the boss of NVIDIA had any reason to meet.

155
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:17,500
So she went and found him.

156
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:21,600
Jensen Huang isn't hard to meet.

157
00:07:21,600 --> 00:07:27,400
The former Denny's dishwasher now runs a company worth about $4.5 trillion,

158
00:07:27,400 --> 00:07:30,100
and he still treats every customer like a regular.

159
00:07:30,100 --> 00:07:31,600
As his spokesman later put it,

160
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:34,500
Meetings with customers are part of a CEO's job.

161
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:37,800
Jensen talks with thousands of customer executives every year.

162
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:40,800
She was about to become one of those thousands.

163
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:43,300
June 2024 in Taipei.

164
00:07:43,300 --> 00:07:46,200
The big chip show Computex has just ended,

165
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:49,400
and Jensen goes for whiskey with people from the AI world.

166
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:50,700
Someone takes photos.

167
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:53,500
In them, right beside him is Alice Huang.

168
00:07:53,500 --> 00:07:54,800
Who haven't I met?

169
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:55,500
Alice.

170
00:07:55,500 --> 00:07:56,600
Megaspeed.

171
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:57,400
Singapore.

172
00:07:57,400 --> 00:07:58,500
Singapore.

173
00:07:58,500 --> 00:07:59,600
What are you building?

174
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:00,900
A cloud company.

175
00:08:00,900 --> 00:08:02,500
Something big.

176
00:08:02,500 --> 00:08:04,200
Then welcome to the table.

177
00:08:04,200 --> 00:08:06,500
Nobody looks at those photos twice.

178
00:08:06,500 --> 00:08:08,000
Later, someone will.

179
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:12,300
That same month, at Megaspeed's Malaysian arm, the paperwork starts.

180
00:08:12,300 --> 00:08:14,900
What do we put up as collateral?

181
00:08:14,900 --> 00:08:16,000
Everything.

182
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,300
The whole Malaysian business?

183
00:08:18,300 --> 00:08:19,600
The whole business.

184
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:21,200
Every last desk.

185
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:22,700
And the lender is?

186
00:08:22,700 --> 00:08:24,200
Owned by Alibaba.

187
00:08:24,200 --> 00:08:25,700
And the buying began.

188
00:08:25,700 --> 00:08:27,600
A Singapore company wants servers.

189
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:28,200
How many?

190
00:08:28,200 --> 00:08:28,900
Yes.

191
00:08:28,900 --> 00:08:30,100
Shipment 100.

192
00:08:30,100 --> 00:08:31,700
Shipment 150.

193
00:08:31,700 --> 00:08:33,000
Shipment 200.

194
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:33,900
What's in them?

195
00:08:33,900 --> 00:08:35,200
Vidya's best.

196
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:37,200
Nearly $2 billion worth.

197
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:39,300
At $30,000 a chip?

198
00:08:39,300 --> 00:08:41,500
That's about 60,000 chips.

199
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:44,300
A parking lot of 60,000 new cars.

200
00:08:44,300 --> 00:08:45,400
In one year.

201
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:49,100
End of 2023, we had $33 million.

202
00:08:49,100 --> 00:08:49,900
Yes.

203
00:08:49,900 --> 00:08:53,100
End of 2024, we have $3 billion.

204
00:08:53,100 --> 00:08:53,800
Yes.

205
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:56,600
$2.9 billion of it is deposits.

206
00:08:56,600 --> 00:08:57,400
From who?

207
00:08:57,400 --> 00:08:58,500
Refundable.

208
00:08:58,500 --> 00:09:00,000
From refundable.

209
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:03,000
About 90 times bigger in one year.

210
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:09,600
If a lemonade stand went from $33 to $3,000 in a year, its parents would have questions.

211
00:09:09,600 --> 00:09:14,200
Then, late in 2024, an official from America's Commerce Department

212
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:16,600
walks into one of the Malaysian data centers.

213
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:19,700
It's supposed to be full of humming AI servers.

214
00:09:19,700 --> 00:09:21,300
Where's the data center?

215
00:09:21,300 --> 00:09:22,900
You're standing in it.

216
00:09:22,900 --> 00:09:24,600
These are boxes.

217
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:27,000
Very advanced boxes.

218
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
I'm opening an investigation.

219
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
The inspector wrote it all down.

220
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:32,600
And nothing happened.

221
00:09:32,600 --> 00:09:35,000
Because on paper, nothing looked wrong.

222
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,500
Every sale went to Singapore or Malaysia, not China.

223
00:09:38,500 --> 00:09:43,300
When Nvidia sent people to check her sites, every chip was exactly where it should be.

224
00:09:43,300 --> 00:09:46,200
Jensen later said they had counted everyone.

225
00:09:46,200 --> 00:09:47,400
That was the whole trick.

226
00:09:47,400 --> 00:09:51,000
The chip stayed put and China rented them over the internet.

227
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:56,000
What almost nobody knew was that American spies were already listening to her.

228
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:01,300
When American spy agencies listen to a foreign target and that target talks to an American,

229
00:10:01,300 --> 00:10:03,500
the reports hide the American's name.

230
00:10:03,500 --> 00:10:05,600
They just say, an American citizen.

231
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:07,900
She's talking to an American citizen.

232
00:10:07,900 --> 00:10:09,600
Then request the name.

233
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:11,100
It's Jensen Huang.

234
00:10:11,100 --> 00:10:12,700
Does he know who she is?

235
00:10:12,700 --> 00:10:13,500
Our read?

236
00:10:13,500 --> 00:10:13,900
No.

237
00:10:13,900 --> 00:10:15,900
How far back does it go?

238
00:10:15,900 --> 00:10:17,700
June, 2024.

239
00:10:17,700 --> 00:10:19,500
A whiskey bar in Taipei.

240
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:20,700
Then we watch.

241
00:10:22,900 --> 00:10:25,200
So where did all those servers come from?

242
00:10:25,200 --> 00:10:30,000
Mostly from Aivris, the American arm of Insper, a Chinese server giant,

243
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:33,800
partly owned by the Chinese state and on America's blacklist.

244
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:34,600
Insper?

245
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:35,900
You're on the list.

246
00:10:35,900 --> 00:10:37,900
I'm Aivris, the American son.

247
00:10:37,900 --> 00:10:39,200
Aivris.

248
00:10:39,200 --> 00:10:40,300
Not on here.

249
00:10:40,300 --> 00:10:41,000
Thanks.

250
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:44,600
December, 2024 to January, 2026.

251
00:10:44,600 --> 00:10:47,600
$4.6 billion of equipment in.

252
00:10:47,600 --> 00:10:49,700
And from Aivris?

253
00:10:49,700 --> 00:10:50,900
$4 billion.

254
00:10:50,900 --> 00:10:55,000
Malaysia was the older cousin who buys beer for kids who are too young,

255
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,700
except this cousin never hands the beer over.

256
00:10:57,700 --> 00:10:59,500
He lets you drink it at his place.

257
00:10:59,500 --> 00:11:05,600
Trade records later counted at least $4.8 billion of AI gear with Nvidia inside,

258
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:08,300
all going to her company's Malaysian sister firm.

259
00:11:08,300 --> 00:11:10,500
Nearly $5 billion.

260
00:11:10,500 --> 00:11:13,500
To one company most people had never heard of.

261
00:11:13,500 --> 00:11:17,500
Real smugglers hid Nvidia chips next to live lobsters.

262
00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:24,100
America's big smuggling bust in December, 2025 was worth $160 million,

263
00:11:24,100 --> 00:11:26,500
about 3% of Megaspeed's haul.

264
00:11:26,500 --> 00:11:28,600
Megaspeed didn't need lobsters.

265
00:11:28,600 --> 00:11:29,800
It had invoices.

266
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:30,400
Cost?

267
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:31,600
Some dinners.

268
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:32,500
Some whiskey.

269
00:11:32,500 --> 00:11:33,200
Return?

270
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:37,200
$4.8 billion of AI gear our companies can use.

271
00:11:37,200 --> 00:11:40,400
And a seat next to the man who argues with Washington.

272
00:11:40,400 --> 00:11:43,500
Did he ever slip you anything under the table?

273
00:11:43,500 --> 00:11:44,800
Not once.

274
00:11:44,800 --> 00:11:48,600
Why pass chips under the table when you can rent the whole warehouse?

275
00:11:48,600 --> 00:11:50,000
We make friends.

276
00:11:52,100 --> 00:11:54,500
So what was all of this really for?

277
00:11:54,500 --> 00:11:56,600
The seat mattered more than the chips.

278
00:11:56,600 --> 00:11:57,800
Not for blueprints.

279
00:11:57,800 --> 00:12:02,600
Nvidia says customers get zero information relevant to national security.

280
00:12:02,600 --> 00:12:04,200
The prize was the man,

281
00:12:04,200 --> 00:12:08,000
because Washington couldn't make up its mind about what China could buy.

282
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:09,100
Your China chip?

283
00:12:09,100 --> 00:12:09,700
Blocked.

284
00:12:09,700 --> 00:12:11,500
So we can't sell it?

285
00:12:11,500 --> 00:12:12,200
August.

286
00:12:12,200 --> 00:12:12,900
You can.

287
00:12:12,900 --> 00:12:14,400
We take 15%.

288
00:12:14,400 --> 00:12:15,600
So we can sell it?

289
00:12:15,600 --> 00:12:16,300
December.

290
00:12:16,300 --> 00:12:17,800
And the stronger one too.

291
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:20,100
We take 25%.

292
00:12:20,100 --> 00:12:22,200
I'm going to need a bigger whiteboard.

293
00:12:22,200 --> 00:12:23,200
Blocked.

294
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:24,700
Then 15%.

295
00:12:24,700 --> 00:12:26,300
Then 25%.

296
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:29,400
Washington changes its mind every few months.

297
00:12:29,400 --> 00:12:31,800
And who does Washington keep hearing from?

298
00:12:31,800 --> 00:12:32,300
Him.

299
00:12:32,300 --> 00:12:36,800
That first call alone cost Nvidia $4.5 billion.

300
00:12:36,800 --> 00:12:38,800
In chips, it suddenly couldn't sell.

301
00:12:38,800 --> 00:12:42,000
That's about $13 from every person in America,

302
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:43,800
gone in one phone call.

303
00:12:43,800 --> 00:12:45,700
And through every one of those calls,

304
00:12:45,700 --> 00:12:48,900
Jensen made the same argument he'd been making for years,

305
00:12:48,900 --> 00:12:51,500
out loud on every stage he could find.

306
00:12:51,500 --> 00:12:54,200
Let American chips compete everywhere.

307
00:12:54,200 --> 00:12:56,100
So you want to sell to China?

308
00:12:56,100 --> 00:12:59,500
I want the whole world building on American chips.

309
00:12:59,500 --> 00:13:00,600
Hold that thought.

310
00:13:00,600 --> 00:13:04,200
In Taipei, in May 2025, he put it bluntly.

311
00:13:04,200 --> 00:13:07,000
All in all, the export control was a failure.

312
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:08,900
A week later to his investors.

313
00:13:08,900 --> 00:13:11,100
The $50 billion dollar China market

314
00:13:11,100 --> 00:13:13,400
is effectively closed to US industry.

315
00:13:13,400 --> 00:13:15,300
$50 billion.

316
00:13:15,300 --> 00:13:19,400
That's about $150 for every American left on the table.

317
00:13:19,400 --> 00:13:20,700
That autumn in London,

318
00:13:20,700 --> 00:13:23,800
he warned that China was going to win the AI race

319
00:13:23,800 --> 00:13:26,200
with cheaper power and fewer rules.

320
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:28,500
That same night, he made sure nobody missed

321
00:13:28,500 --> 00:13:29,700
whose side he was on.

322
00:13:29,700 --> 00:13:32,600
It's vital that America wins by racing ahead

323
00:13:32,600 --> 00:13:34,500
and winning developers worldwide.

324
00:13:34,500 --> 00:13:36,400
That's Jensen in one line.

325
00:13:36,500 --> 00:13:37,900
He wants America to win

326
00:13:37,900 --> 00:13:41,500
and he wants to sell the whole world the tools to build on it.

327
00:13:41,500 --> 00:13:43,100
Now look at it from Beijing.

328
00:13:43,100 --> 00:13:46,200
One chip is worth about $30,000.

329
00:13:46,200 --> 00:13:49,000
One conversation between this man and the White House

330
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:52,700
can open a $50 billion market or close it.

331
00:13:52,700 --> 00:13:55,200
Which one would you rather have a seat next to?

332
00:13:55,200 --> 00:13:56,900
What is he telling Washington?

333
00:13:56,900 --> 00:13:59,600
Let American chips compete everywhere.

334
00:13:59,600 --> 00:14:02,600
He says that on every stage in the world.

335
00:14:02,600 --> 00:14:04,000
Why do we need you?

336
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,000
Because I hear it first.

337
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:07,900
And I see who he says it to.

338
00:14:07,900 --> 00:14:10,200
That's the job the memo would later describe.

339
00:14:10,200 --> 00:14:11,400
Learn what he knows

340
00:14:11,400 --> 00:14:14,100
and sit beside the man Washington listens to.

341
00:14:14,100 --> 00:14:15,900
And Washington kept moving.

342
00:14:15,900 --> 00:14:17,700
In December 2025,

343
00:14:17,700 --> 00:14:21,200
Washington let approved Chinese buyers have the stronger chip.

344
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:22,200
That same week,

345
00:14:22,200 --> 00:14:24,900
prosecutors announced that big smuggling bust.

346
00:14:24,900 --> 00:14:26,900
The one mega speed made looks small.

347
00:14:26,900 --> 00:14:29,600
You're under arrest for sending these to China.

348
00:14:29,600 --> 00:14:30,400
Approved.

349
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:32,000
What did you just approve?

350
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:33,500
Sending these to China.

351
00:14:33,500 --> 00:14:36,700
Even the approved buyers weren't always what they seemed.

352
00:14:36,700 --> 00:14:39,000
New Matrix, Beijing approved.

353
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:40,200
Hello, anyone?

354
00:14:40,200 --> 00:14:42,600
The New York Times found that New Matrix,

355
00:14:42,600 --> 00:14:46,600
a 17-month-old firm Washington had cleared to handle those chips,

356
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:49,500
had an office that was a hollow gray shell

357
00:14:49,500 --> 00:14:51,600
with wires dangling from the ceiling.

358
00:14:51,600 --> 00:14:53,400
On paper, it was approved.

359
00:14:53,400 --> 00:14:56,000
On paper, mega speed looked fine too.

360
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:59,900
Back in the middle of 2025, mega speed had gone quiet.

361
00:14:59,900 --> 00:15:01,100
It stopped buying

362
00:15:01,100 --> 00:15:05,300
and walked away from an order worth $3.2 billion.

363
00:15:05,300 --> 00:15:07,200
In October 2025,

364
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:11,100
Senator Tom Cotton asked Commerce to investigate mega speed.

365
00:15:11,100 --> 00:15:13,100
He called the company it came from.

366
00:15:13,100 --> 00:15:15,800
A Chinese government-owned cloud company.

367
00:15:15,800 --> 00:15:19,400
The New York Times found the $2 billion of chips.

368
00:15:19,400 --> 00:15:23,100
Nvidia said mega speed had no China shareholders.

369
00:15:23,100 --> 00:15:25,100
In February 2026,

370
00:15:25,100 --> 00:15:26,700
her data center landlord,

371
00:15:26,700 --> 00:15:28,300
a business of Bain Capital,

372
00:15:28,300 --> 00:15:31,000
gave all the power it had set aside for mega speed

373
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:32,100
to someone else.

374
00:15:32,100 --> 00:15:34,900
And the Commerce case moved to the Justice Department.

375
00:15:34,900 --> 00:15:37,300
By then, the people listening to her phone

376
00:15:37,300 --> 00:15:39,400
had a lot more than boxes.

377
00:15:41,600 --> 00:15:43,600
They had watched her for about a year.

378
00:15:43,600 --> 00:15:45,700
They photographed the dinners in Singapore.

379
00:15:45,700 --> 00:15:47,400
In May 2025,

380
00:15:47,400 --> 00:15:50,200
there's even video of the two of them leaving a dinner

381
00:15:50,200 --> 00:15:52,200
with AI suppliers in Taipei.

382
00:15:52,200 --> 00:15:54,100
Alice, a few feet behind him.

383
00:15:54,100 --> 00:15:56,000
In the spring of 2026,

384
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:57,600
America wrote it down.

385
00:15:57,600 --> 00:16:01,200
In a memo overseen by Secretary of State Marco Rubio.

386
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:04,000
Recruited by China's Ministry of State Security

387
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,200
to gather information on Jensen Huang

388
00:16:06,200 --> 00:16:09,000
and to shape his lobbying for chip sales to China.

389
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:10,600
Who else sees this?

390
00:16:10,600 --> 00:16:11,800
Just this table.

391
00:16:11,800 --> 00:16:13,900
The memo went round that small group

392
00:16:13,900 --> 00:16:16,700
before President Trump's trip to Beijing in May.

393
00:16:16,700 --> 00:16:18,300
According to the New York Times,

394
00:16:18,300 --> 00:16:21,000
the president had been briefed and didn't act.

395
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,700
Jensen had been left off the list for that plane.

396
00:16:23,700 --> 00:16:25,000
Then, Trump called him

397
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:26,600
and Jensen flew to Alaska

398
00:16:26,600 --> 00:16:29,300
to climb aboard Air Force One on the way.

399
00:16:31,300 --> 00:16:34,200
On the 29th of October, 2026,

400
00:16:34,200 --> 00:16:36,100
the New York Times broke the story.

401
00:16:36,100 --> 00:16:39,000
Alice Huang had been passing information to Beijing.

402
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,100
And American intelligence knew it.

403
00:16:41,100 --> 00:16:43,600
NVIDIA's stock fell about 3%.

404
00:16:43,600 --> 00:16:45,000
Jensen told Bloomberg

405
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,200
her contacts were about mega-speed.

406
00:16:47,200 --> 00:16:49,900
It was all completely normal and not unusual.

407
00:16:49,900 --> 00:16:51,800
He said she no longer worked there.

408
00:16:51,800 --> 00:16:53,200
Beijing answered too.

409
00:16:53,200 --> 00:16:56,100
Nothing more than an attempt to smear China.

410
00:16:56,100 --> 00:16:58,100
Three congressmen asked Jensen in

411
00:16:58,100 --> 00:17:01,200
for a closed-door briefing on the 18th of November.

412
00:17:01,200 --> 00:17:02,600
Their letter warned of

413
00:17:02,600 --> 00:17:05,500
significant national security concerns.

414
00:17:05,500 --> 00:17:07,000
On the 6th of November,

415
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:08,700
the memo itself leaked.

416
00:17:08,700 --> 00:17:10,000
The spy memo's out!

417
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:10,700
I know.

418
00:17:10,700 --> 00:17:12,100
How bad is it?

419
00:17:12,100 --> 00:17:14,700
We're up 2.78%.

420
00:17:14,700 --> 00:17:15,200
Up?

421
00:17:15,200 --> 00:17:17,700
Today's news will not slow NVIDIA.

422
00:17:17,700 --> 00:17:20,300
But here's the part that still has no answer.

423
00:17:20,300 --> 00:17:22,400
Why did nobody tell Jensen?

424
00:17:22,500 --> 00:17:25,400
When the government finds a spy close to an American,

425
00:17:25,400 --> 00:17:26,600
there's a way to tell them.

426
00:17:26,600 --> 00:17:28,700
It's called a defensive briefing.

427
00:17:28,700 --> 00:17:33,100
In 2015, the FBI gave one to a congressman in California.

428
00:17:33,100 --> 00:17:36,000
Your fundraiser works for Chinese intelligence.

429
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:37,300
Then she's gone.

430
00:17:37,300 --> 00:17:38,400
He cut her off.

431
00:17:38,400 --> 00:17:40,500
Jensen Huang never got that visit.

432
00:17:40,500 --> 00:17:42,900
The people who knew unmasked his name,

433
00:17:42,900 --> 00:17:44,200
photographed his dinners,

434
00:17:44,200 --> 00:17:45,100
wrote the memo,

435
00:17:45,100 --> 00:17:46,600
and briefed the president.

436
00:17:46,600 --> 00:17:48,700
Nobody told the man at the table.

437
00:17:48,700 --> 00:17:50,300
He had no idea.

438
00:17:50,400 --> 00:17:51,400
And the memo says

439
00:17:51,400 --> 00:17:53,800
the friendship became more than a friendship.

440
00:17:53,800 --> 00:17:54,900
He was the target,

441
00:17:54,900 --> 00:17:57,700
and she got closer than anyone was ever meant to.

442
00:17:57,700 --> 00:17:59,100
On the 8th of November,

443
00:17:59,100 --> 00:18:00,700
Jensen wrote to his staff.

444
00:18:00,700 --> 00:18:03,700
I owe my family and all of you an apology.

445
00:18:03,700 --> 00:18:05,300
I was unfaithful to my wife.

446
00:18:05,300 --> 00:18:07,500
My actions hurt her and our children.

447
00:18:07,500 --> 00:18:09,400
There is no excuse for what I did.

448
00:18:09,400 --> 00:18:10,600
I am deeply sorry.

449
00:18:10,600 --> 00:18:14,000
NVIDIA's board set up a committee of independent directors

450
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:15,400
with outside lawyers.

451
00:18:15,400 --> 00:18:16,900
So far, it sees.

452
00:18:16,900 --> 00:18:18,200
No reason to believe that

453
00:18:18,200 --> 00:18:22,100
either national security or NVIDIA secrets were compromised.

454
00:18:22,100 --> 00:18:24,000
Jensen is still CEO.

455
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,700
And here's where his story reaches your wallet.

456
00:18:26,700 --> 00:18:30,000
NVIDIA is now about $8 of every hundred

457
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,300
in an S&P 500 index fund.

458
00:18:32,300 --> 00:18:33,800
If you have a retirement account,

459
00:18:33,800 --> 00:18:35,800
there's a good chance you own a slice of him.

460
00:18:35,800 --> 00:18:37,700
Wait, I own NVIDIA?

461
00:18:37,700 --> 00:18:39,300
Everybody owns NVIDIA.

462
00:18:39,300 --> 00:18:42,900
And one scary headline from China can move it hard.

463
00:18:42,900 --> 00:18:44,800
In January 2025,

464
00:18:44,800 --> 00:18:48,600
a Chinese AI lab called DeepSeek shocked Wall Street.

465
00:18:48,600 --> 00:18:50,900
A Chinese lab built a cheap AI.

466
00:18:50,900 --> 00:18:54,600
We just lost $589 billion.

467
00:18:54,600 --> 00:18:56,000
How much is that?

468
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:59,600
About $1,700 for every person in America.

469
00:18:59,600 --> 00:19:03,100
It was the biggest one day loss in market history.

470
00:19:03,100 --> 00:19:06,500
NVIDIA reports its earnings on the 17th of November.

471
00:19:06,500 --> 00:19:08,900
The congressmen get their briefing the next day.

472
00:19:08,900 --> 00:19:10,100
And two weeks later,

473
00:19:10,100 --> 00:19:12,500
Jensen walks onto a stage in Washington,

474
00:19:12,600 --> 00:19:15,500
the same city as the people who never told him.

475
00:19:15,500 --> 00:19:18,100
And Alice Huang, she's gone from mega speed.

476
00:19:18,100 --> 00:19:20,800
Her name has been taken off its company papers.

477
00:19:20,800 --> 00:19:22,700
Nobody has said where she is.

478
00:19:22,700 --> 00:19:24,600
Go back to that dinner in Singapore,

479
00:19:24,600 --> 00:19:26,900
the candles, the toast to friends.

480
00:19:26,900 --> 00:19:29,300
The chair next to Jensen is empty now.

481
00:19:29,300 --> 00:19:30,400
We make friends.

482
00:19:30,400 --> 00:19:34,100
America watched her at his table for almost two years

483
00:19:34,100 --> 00:19:35,300
and never told him.

484
00:19:35,300 --> 00:19:37,800
So who's pulling up a chair next to the people

485
00:19:37,800 --> 00:19:40,000
who build America's AI tonight?

486
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,200
Jensen ended his letter like this.

487
00:19:42,200 --> 00:19:44,300
I love our country and our company.

488
00:19:44,300 --> 00:19:47,000
I am proud and grateful to be an American.

